<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · monument</p><h1 class="enc-title">Santa Maria delle Grazie, Milan</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1463">1463</a>–<a href="/years/1497">1497</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/15th-century">15th century</a> · Also known as Santa Maria delle Grazie</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Santa Maria delle Grazie is a Dominican church and convent in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/milan">Milan</a>, built from 1463 to designs by Guiniforte Solari. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ludovico-sforza">Ludovico Sforza</a> had its east end rebuilt with a large domed tribune, attributed to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/donato-bramante">Donato Bramante</a>, as a family mausoleum. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/leonardo-da-vinci">Leonardo da Vinci</a> painted the Last Supper on the wall of its refectory around 1495–1498, and the site is a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/unesco-world-heritage-site">UNESCO World Heritage Site</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/santa-maria-delle-grazie-milan.jpg" alt="Santa Maria delle Grazie, Milan" width="320" height="173" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Marcin Białek · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Santa_Maria_delle_Grazie_Milan_2013.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
